# Summary
Introduced: SSE‑C headers on POST are no longer rejected and are ignored because POST only parses SSE‑C form fields.
The new SSE‑C POST implementation derives the encryption context exclusively from multipart form fields. The previous explicit rejection of SSE‑C headers on POST has been removed, but no header parsing was added for POST. As a result, POST requests that supply SSE‑C via headers (common for S3 clients) are accepted and stored with ObjectEncryption::None. This can silently drop at‑rest encryption for clients expecting SSE‑C, leading to plaintext storage and potential confidentiality exposure.

