## Report
Attempted dynamic reproduction by adding a PoC test (crates/s3-tests/tests/post_object_sse_c_headers.rs) and running `cargo test -p s3-tests --test post_object_sse_c_headers -- --nocapture`, but the build failed because the ec-sys crate requires libisal: `libisal not found; install intel-isa-l or set PKG_CONFIG_PATH` (see /workspace/validation_artifacts/post_object_sse_c_headers_output.txt). Valgrind and gdb are unavailable in this environment (`valgrind: command not found`, `gdb: command not found`), so crash/valgrind/debugger validation could not proceed. Code inspection confirms the regression: the streaming POST path derives SSE-C only from form fields (`parse_sse_customer_form_fields(form_fields)` in prepare_streaming_post_object, then sets encryption to ObjectEncryption::None when absent; mod.rs:2180-2197). The SSE-C form-field parser only reads multipart fields (mod.rs:3134-3141), and no POST path consults request headers. Header-based SSE-C parsing exists separately for other operations (`parse_sse_customer_request_with_names` uses req.header; mod.rs:3039-3064), so header-only SSE-C on POST is ignored. The commit diff also shows removal of `ensure_sse_customer_not_requested` from POST handling, so SSE-C headers are no longer rejected. This matches the finding: POST requests that supply SSE-C headers but no form fields will be accepted and stored unencrypted.

